MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m10",
  "summary": "Understand lead-acid batteries, alternator charging systems and starter circuits, and test, diagnose and service them safely.",
  "outcomes": ["Describe battery construction, cell voltage, capacity and cold-cranking amps, and how to read state of charge.","Test and care for a battery, jump start a vehicle correctly and measure a parasitic drain.","Explain how an alternator generates and regulates charging current.","Diagnose no-charge, overcharge and noisy-alternator faults using voltage, current and drop tests.","Describe the starter motor, solenoid and starting circuit and how they work together.","Diagnose slow cranking, click-only, no-crank and spin-without-engaging faults."],
  "cheatsheet": ["A 12 V lead-acid battery has six cells of about 2.1 V each. Rested and full, it reads about 12.6 V or more.","Batteries give off hydrogen, which is explosive, and contain sulphuric acid. Wear eye protection and keep flames away.","Jump start: red to dead positive, red to donor positive, black to donor negative, last black to a bare metal earth on the dead car.","Alternator output is typically about 13.8 to 14.7 V with the engine running. Check the manual.","A battery that tests fine but keeps going flat points to a charging fault or a parasitic drain.","Cranking voltage on a healthy system should stay above about 9.6 V at normal temperature.","Click only: weak battery, poor connections or a failed solenoid contact. Check voltage at the starter while cranking.","Spin without engaging: the starter drive or pinion is faulty or the ring gear is damaged.","Stop-start vehicles need an EFB or AGM battery, and often a new battery must be registered with the vehicle."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m10-l1", "title": "The lead-acid battery", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The battery supplies the starter motor, stores electrical energy and steadies the voltage for the whole electrical system. Most vehicle batteries are lead-acid types. This lesson explains how they are built and how to describe them."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"battery-construction"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Construction"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A 12 V battery is made of six **cells** connected in series. Each cell has plates of lead (the negative plate) and lead dioxide (the positive plate) in a bath of dilute sulphuric acid called the **electrolyte**. Separators between the plates stop them touching. Each cell gives about 2.1 V, so six cells give about 12.6 V. During discharge the acid reacts with the plates and the electrolyte becomes weaker. During charging, the reaction runs backwards."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Capacity and cold-cranking amps"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Capacity (Ah, amp-hours):** how much current the battery can supply for a given time. A 60 Ah battery can supply about 3 A for 20 hours.","**Cold-cranking amps (CCA):** the current that a battery can deliver at low temperature (usually −18 °C) for a short time while keeping the voltage above a set level. A higher CCA suits a larger or diesel engine."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Always fit a battery with at least the capacity and CCA that the manufacturer specifies, and the right size, terminal layout and fixing."},
      {"t":"h","text":"State of charge by voltage"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Switch off everything and let the battery rest for a few hours after charging or driving. Then measure the open-circuit voltage."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Rested voltage","Approximate state of charge"],"rows":[["12.6 V or more","100%"],["12.4 V","About 75%"],["12.2 V","About 50%"],["12.0 V","About 25%"],["11.8 V or less","Flat"]],"caption":"Rested battery voltage and charge (typical values)"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Battery types"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Flooded (conventional):** liquid electrolyte, may need topping up with distilled water on non-sealed types.","**EFB (enhanced flooded battery):** stronger and better at repeated charging and discharging, used in many stop-start vehicles.","**AGM (absorbent glass mat):** the electrolyte is held in glass mats, sealed and spill-resistant. Used for stop-start and heavy electrical loads."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case, terminals and fitting"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The battery case is moulded plastic with a lid welded on, and has six cell compartments. Cell connectors join the cells in series, and a vent, often with a flame arrester, lets gas escape safely, sometimes through a tube to the outside of the vehicle. Terminal posts are lead, and come in different sizes. A larger positive post is the usual rule, to prevent reversal, and some batteries use special side or top terminals. A battery must be correct for the vehicle in four ways: size and fixing, terminal type and position, polarity (which side the positive is on) and capacity."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check the date code. A battery is best used within a few months of manufacture, and one left on a shelf for a year may be partly sulphated already.","Fit the hold-down firmly. A loose battery shakes its plates to pieces and can short against the body.","Connect the positive terminal first on fitting, and the negative last. Do not use a hammer to push a clamp on.","Coat terminals with a thin film of grease or terminal protector after tightening."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Batteries give off **hydrogen** gas while charging, and it explodes if sparked. The acid burns the skin and eyes and ruins clothes. Wear eye protection and gloves, keep flames and sparks away, and charge in a ventilated area. If acid gets on your skin or in your eyes, rinse with a lot of clean water at once and get medical help."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Lift a battery by its carrier or with both hands at the ends. A battery is heavy and the case can crack if you squeeze it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l2", "title": "Battery chemistry, ratings and failure", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A battery is a chemical machine. Understanding the chemistry and what it does under load, in the cold and when abused, helps you to choose the right battery, test it properly and explain failures. This lesson covers the cell reactions, the ratings and the main ways in which a battery fails."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How a cell works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The positive plate is lead dioxide, the negative plate is spongy lead, and the electrolyte is dilute sulphuric acid. On discharge, both plates react with the acid and become **lead sulphate**, and the acid becomes weaker, turning into water. This is why the specific gravity of the electrolyte falls as a battery discharges. On charge, a current drives the reactions backwards: the plates revert to lead dioxide and lead, and the acid becomes stronger. A cell produces about 2.1 V when full. It falls to about 1.75 V when empty."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Plates, grids and separators"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Each plate is a lead alloy **grid** that holds active paste. Thin plates give a large surface area and a high cranking current, but wear sooner than thick plates, which suit deep-cycle use. Grid alloys include calcium and antimony. Calcium alloys lose less water and self-discharge slowly, so they are used in maintenance-free batteries. **Separators**, thin porous sheets of plastic, keep the plates from touching and let the acid pass. A damaged separator or lumps of shed paste can short a cell."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ratings explained"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Rating","What it means","Why it matters"],"rows":[["Capacity (Ah)","Amp-hours it can supply for a set time, often 20 hours, to a set end voltage","How long it can run loads with the engine off"],["Cold-cranking amps (CCA)","Current at −18 °C for 30 seconds that holds the battery above 7.2 V","Ability to start a cold engine. A diesel needs more than a petrol engine."],["Reserve capacity (RC)","Minutes it can supply 25 A at 27 °C to a final voltage of 10.5 V","How long it can run the vehicle if the alternator fails"],["Dimensions and terminal layout","Case size, hold-down shape, terminal positions and polarity","The battery must fit and the cables must reach with the right polarity"]],"caption":"Different standards give different CCA figures for the same battery. Compare ratings of the same standard."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Temperature effects"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Battery temperature","Approximate available capacity"],"rows":[["27 °C","100%"],["0 °C","About 65 to 70%"],["−18 °C","About 40 to 50%"]],"caption":"In the cold the chemistry is slower and the engine is harder to turn, so the margin shrinks both ways."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Heat is also a battery's enemy. High temperatures speed up the chemical reactions that wear the grids out, and water is lost faster. In hot climates a battery often lasts only two to three years. A battery fitted away from the engine heat, or with a heat shield, lasts longer. A fully discharged battery can freeze at around −10 °C, because the electrolyte is mostly water, while a fully charged one will not freeze until around −60 °C."},
      {"t":"h","text":"How batteries fail"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Failure","Cause","Signs"],"rows":[["Sulphation","Left discharged, plates become coated with hard lead sulphate crystals","Will not accept charge, low capacity, high voltage on charge"],["Grid corrosion","Age, heat and overcharging","Loss of capacity, internal break"],["Shedding paste","Vibration and deep cycling","Sludge in the bottom of the case, a shorted cell"],["Short cell","Damaged separator or sediment bridging the plates","One cell low in the hydrometer test, voltage under 10.6 V"],["Dry cells","Overcharging, heat, no topping up","Exposed plates, hot battery, weak starting"],["Cracked case","Frost, impact or loose hold-down","Leaks and corrosion round the base"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Looking after a battery"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Keep it clean and dry, with tight terminals and a firm hold-down.","Keep it charged. A battery left flat for weeks sulphates. If a vehicle is parked for long periods, use a maintainer or disconnect the battery.","Top up non-sealed batteries with distilled water only, to just cover the plates, and never overfill.","Check the charging voltage and fix problems at once.","Replace a weak battery before it leaves a customer stranded, and replace it with the same type, size and rating."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Most batteries do not fail suddenly. They lose capacity slowly, and then a cold morning shows it. Test the battery under load whenever the vehicle is serviced."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never use tap water, acid from a different source or a metal funnel. Add only distilled water, and handle acid as in module 1. Never tip a battery on its side."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l3", "title": "Battery testing and care", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A battery that is correctly tested and cared for lasts longer and prevents many starting problems. This lesson covers testing, charging and jump starting."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Visual check"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Check the case for cracks and swelling, the terminals for corrosion and tightness, the hold-down clamp and the age. Look for signs of leaking acid. Clean corrosion with a mixture of baking soda and water, rinse and dry, then smear the clamps with a thin film of grease. Never overtighten the clamps."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Open-circuit voltage:** measure with everything off and the battery rested.","**Load test:** apply a load as recommended (typically about half the CCA rating) for around 15 seconds. The voltage should stay above about 9.6 V.","**Conductance test:** a handheld tester that sends a small signal to estimate the battery's health and CCA. It is quick and does not drain the battery."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"A battery with good open-circuit voltage but poor load performance is worn out. A battery that is flat may need charging before it can be tested."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing with a hydrometer"},
      {"t":"p","text":"On a battery with removable caps, a **hydrometer** measures the **specific gravity** (SG) of the electrolyte in each cell, which shows how charged it is. Draw some acid into the hydrometer until the float rises, and read the scale at the surface level. Return the acid to the cell and test each cell in turn. Wear eye protection and gloves, and rinse the instrument with water afterwards."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Specific gravity (at about 25 °C)","State of charge"],"rows":[["1.265 to 1.280","Fully charged"],["1.225","About 75%"],["1.190","About 50%"],["1.155","About 25%"],["1.120 or less","Discharged"]],"caption":"Readings rise and fall with temperature, so correct them as the instrument instructions say."},
      {"t":"p","text":"All the cells in a good battery should agree within about 0.025. A cell that is 0.050 or more below the others has a shorted plate or a damaged separator, and the battery is faulty. A battery with a low SG in all cells needs charging. If it will not hold the charge, the plates are sulphated or worn."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charging"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Use a charger suited to the battery type and set to the right voltage. Remove the battery cables first if the charger instructions require it, or at least switch everything off. Connect the charger leads before you switch on the charger. A slow charge, at a tenth of the capacity or less, is gentler on the battery than a fast charge. Never charge a frozen battery."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Jump starting"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch off both vehicles and put them in neutral or park. Do not let the vehicles touch.","Connect the first red clamp to the positive terminal of the flat battery.","Connect the other red clamp to the positive terminal of the donor battery.","Connect the first black clamp to the negative terminal of the donor battery.","Connect the last black clamp to a bare metal part of the engine or body on the flat vehicle, away from the battery.","Start the donor engine, wait a few minutes, then start the flat vehicle. Remove the clamps in the reverse order."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parasitic drain"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **parasitic drain** is current that flows with the vehicle off. Some drain is normal, to keep memory and clocks, typically below about 50 mA once all the modules have gone to sleep, which can take 20 minutes or more. To measure it, connect a meter in series with the battery negative, using a clamp meter or an ammeter with a suitable range, wait for the modules to sleep, then read. If the drain is too high, remove fuses one at a time and see which circuit makes the current drop."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Disposing of batteries"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Lead-acid batteries contain lead and acid, and must never go in ordinary rubbish. Keep old batteries upright in a tray, away from heat and rain, and send them to a recycler. Most suppliers take old batteries back when you buy a new one, and more than 95% of the lead is recycled. If a battery has leaked, wear gloves, neutralise the spill with baking soda and clean up, following your workshop's spill procedure."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Do not crank the engine while the ammeter is connected in series. The large starter current will blow the meter fuse or damage the meter."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never connect the last jump lead clamp to the negative terminal of the flat battery. A spark there can ignite hydrogen. Use a metal earth point away from the battery."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l4", "title": "Battery charging methods and equipment", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Charging looks simple, but the way it is done affects the life of the battery, and a poor method can ruin it. This lesson explains how chargers work, how long a charge takes and how to treat different battery types."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charging principles"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A charger must push current into the battery against its own voltage. It must therefore supply a voltage higher than the battery's open-circuit voltage, in practice about 13.8 to 14.8 V for a 12 V battery. The current is limited, because a deeply discharged battery would otherwise take a huge current and overheat. As the battery becomes charged, its voltage rises, the current falls, and gassing begins. Too long at a high voltage dries the cells."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Charger type","How it works","Notes"],"rows":[["Simple transformer charger","Fixed or stepped output, no control","Needs watching. Can overcharge if left connected."],["Constant-current charger","Holds the current steady while the voltage rises","Good for slow charging. Needs a timer or monitoring."],["Constant-voltage charger","Holds the voltage fixed, with the current falling as the battery fills","Gentle. Takes longer for a flat battery."],["Smart (multi-stage) charger","Switches automatically between bulk, absorption and float stages, and may test the battery","Best for regular use. Often has settings for AGM, EFB and flooded batteries."],["Boost or fast charger","High current for a short time","For emergencies. Do not use on a deeply discharged or damaged battery, and do not leave it unattended."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The three stages of a smart charge"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Bulk:** a high constant current fills most of the capacity, until the voltage reaches about 14.4 V.","**Absorption:** the voltage is held and the current falls as the battery fills, to finish the charge safely.","**Float:** a lower voltage of about 13.2 to 13.8 V maintains the full charge without overcharging."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How long will it take?"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Time (h) = Ah to replace ÷ charge current (A) × 1.3","caption":"The factor 1.3 allows for the energy lost as heat and gas"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Charging a flat battery","steps":["A 60 Ah battery reads 12.0 V, about 25% charged, so it is missing about 45 Ah (75% of 60).","At a charger setting of 6 A (one tenth of capacity), time = 45 ÷ 6 × 1.3 = 9.75 hours.","With a 10 A fast charge, time = 45 ÷ 10 × 1.3 = 5.9 hours. The battery will warm up faster, and the charger should be monitored.","Check the voltage and specific gravity at the end. A full battery reads 12.6 V or more after resting, with a steady charge current that has stopped falling."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charging different types"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Flooded:** can be charged at up to 14.4 V, with ventilation and a care for gassing.","**AGM:** needs a regulated charge. A maximum voltage of about 14.4 to 14.8 V (follow the maker) avoids drying the mats. Use a charger with an AGM mode.","**EFB:** similar to flooded, but follow the manufacturer's advice.","**Lithium and other types:** need special chargers. Never use a lead-acid charger.","**Frozen battery:** thaw it first, then charge it. Charging a frozen battery can crack the case."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charging safely"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Work in a ventilated area, away from flames, and wear eye protection.","Switch the charger off before you connect or disconnect the clamps.","Connect the red clamp to the battery positive, then the black clamp to the battery negative (or, on a battery that is in the vehicle, to a clean earth point away from the battery).","Set the correct voltage and battery type, and switch on.","Check from time to time. If the battery becomes very hot (above about 50 °C), smells of rotten eggs, or bubbles violently, switch off at once.","Switch off before you remove the clamps."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A slow, controlled charge is gentler and gives a fuller charge than a quick one. Use a smart charger for a battery you want to keep."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never charge a leaking, cracked or visibly swollen battery. Never use a charger that is not designed for the battery type, and never jump start a frozen battery."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l5", "title": "The charging system", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The alternator replaces the energy that the battery gives to the starter and runs the electrical loads while the engine runs. This lesson explains how it works."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"alternator-charging"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Main parts"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Rotor:** a rotating electromagnet, formed by a coil of wire (the field winding) wound on a core. Current reaches the coil through **slip rings** and **brushes**.","**Stator:** a fixed set of three windings around the rotor, in which the rotating magnetic field induces an alternating voltage.","**Rectifier:** a set of six diodes that change the alternating current (AC) from the stator into direct current (DC) for the vehicle.","**Voltage regulator:** controls the current in the rotor to keep the output voltage steady."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"How it works"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The engine turns the rotor by a belt. When the rotor field is energised, its rotating magnetic field cuts the stator windings and produces a three-phase AC voltage. The diodes rectify this to DC. The regulator senses the system voltage and lowers or raises the field current to hold the output at a set value. A stronger field means more output. At low electrical load, the field current is small."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Charging voltage"},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the engine running, the system voltage is typically 13.8 to 14.7 V. It may vary on vehicles whose ECU controls the alternator to save fuel, and in cold weather it can be higher. Check the manual."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Ripple"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The rectified output has a small AC ripple on top of the DC voltage. If a diode fails, ripple increases. A meter set to AC volts across the battery terminals with the engine running should read very little, typically under about 0.5 V. A high reading means a diode problem."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The warning lamp"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The charging warning lamp comes on with the ignition and goes out when the alternator produces voltage. If it stays on, comes on while driving or flickers, there is a charging problem. Some lamps are supplied through the field circuit, so a blown lamp can stop an older alternator from starting to charge."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The charging circuit and its terminals"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Terminal","Usual job"],"rows":[["B+","Main output to the battery, through a heavy cable and sometimes a fusible link"],["D+ or L","Warning lamp and field excitation. Lit lamp supplies the first current to the field."],["S or sense","Reads battery voltage directly so the regulator allows for voltage drop in the cable"],["IG","Ignition-switched supply that wakes the regulator"],["LIN or FR","Data link or load signal to the engine computer on smart systems"]],"caption":"Names and functions differ with the make. Use the wiring diagram."},
      {"t":"p","text":"If an alternator will not start to charge, check the supply to the warning lamp and field terminal with the ignition on. On older systems a broken wire or blown bulb in the lamp circuit means no field current, so no output. On modern systems the engine computer controls the field, so use the scan tool to check for charging codes and to command the alternator."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"An alternator does not charge a flat battery quickly. It is designed to hold a charged battery topped up. A deeply discharged battery should be charged with a proper charger first."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"To test the output quickly, measure the battery voltage with the engine running at about 2,000 rpm and the headlamps, heater and rear window heater on. It should stay above about 13.2 V."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l6", "title": "The alternator in depth: output, regulation and smart charging", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The alternator is a simple machine with a complex job. It must give a steady voltage from the idle speed of an engine to the top speed of a motorway, with loads that change from a few amps to many. This lesson explains the speeds and outputs, the regulation methods and how modern charging systems save fuel."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Speed and output"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The alternator is driven by a belt, usually with a pulley ratio of about 2 to 3 to 1, so it turns two to three times faster than the engine. Alternator ratings are given at a high speed (often 6,000 rpm), but at engine idle the alternator is turning at only about 1,600 to 2,000 rpm and gives much less output."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Alternator speed and output at idle","steps":["The engine idles at 750 rpm. The crankshaft pulley is 150 mm and the alternator pulley is 60 mm.","The ratio is 150 ÷ 60 = 2.5, so the alternator turns at 750 × 2.5 = 1,875 rpm.","A 100 A alternator may give only about 35 to 45 A at this speed, and about 80 A at 3,000 rpm alternator speed.","With the headlamps, heated rear window, fan and wipers on, the load can exceed what the alternator gives at idle, so the battery makes up the difference and voltage falls. This is normal, but a battery that is weak or a belt that slips makes it much worse."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Alternator speed (rpm)","Typical output of a 100 A alternator"],"rows":[["1,500","About 30 A"],["2,000","About 50 A"],["3,000","About 75 A"],["6,000","About 100 A (rated output)"]],"caption":"The exact curve is in the manufacturer's data. It is the basis of the output test."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Windings and rectifier"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The three stator windings are connected in a **star** (wye) or **delta** pattern. A star winding gives more voltage at low speed, and a delta winding more current at high speed. Six main diodes form a **bridge rectifier**, and three smaller diodes, the **diode trio**, supply the field current in some designs. Many alternators also have a **neutral** terminal for tests."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The regulator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The regulator keeps the voltage at its set point by controlling the field current, switching it on and off at high frequency, with a longer on-time when more output is needed. Older designs sense the voltage inside the alternator, and newer ones sense it at the battery or at the fuse box, which is better, because it takes account of voltage drop in the cable. Modern systems are controlled by the engine computer through a **LIN** or **PWM** signal. The computer can request a different voltage depending on the conditions."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Smart charging"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Regenerative charging:** the computer raises the voltage during deceleration, using energy that would be lost, and lowers it during acceleration to relieve the engine of the load.","**Load response control:** raises the field current gradually when a heavy load is switched on, so that engine speed does not dip at idle.","**Temperature compensation:** raises the voltage in the cold, to charge the battery better, and lowers it in the heat to prevent gassing.","**Battery monitoring:** a sensor on the battery negative reports current, voltage and temperature, so the computer knows the state of charge."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"On these vehicles, the battery voltage on a meter can change from about 12.5 V to 15 V as the computer works, which is normal. Do not judge the alternator from a single reading. Use a scan tool to see the commanded and actual values. Fitting the wrong battery or sensor can spoil the strategy."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing an alternator"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Test","How","Good result"],"rows":[["No-load voltage","Battery voltage with the engine at 2,000 rpm, loads off","13.8 to 14.7 V"],["Loaded voltage","Same, with headlamps, fan, heated window on","Above about 13.2 V"],["Output current","Clamp meter on the output cable, loads on, engine at the speed in the manual","Within about 10 to 15% of the rating at that speed"],["AC ripple","Meter on AC volts across the battery","Under about 0.5 V"],["Voltage drop, output side","Alternator output to battery positive, loaded","Under about 0.3 to 0.5 V"],["Voltage drop, earth side","Alternator case to battery negative, loaded","Under about 0.2 to 0.3 V"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Always check the belt, the battery and the cables before you replace an alternator. Many alternators fail only because the battery was deeply discharged and the alternator ran at its limit for weeks."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Check the belt tension and the tensioner. A slipping belt can cut the output at high load, even though the alternator is good."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l7", "title": "Charging faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Charging problems may be a faulty alternator, a loose belt, bad wiring or a poor battery. This lesson gives you a way to tell the difference."},
      {"t":"h","text":"No charge"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Symptoms are a lit charging warning lamp, a flat battery and dimming lights. Check the belt first: it must be present, in good condition and correctly tensioned. Check the connectors and the main output cable. Measure the battery voltage with the engine running. If it is the same as the voltage with the engine off, the alternator is not charging. Check the fuse in the field circuit and the voltage at the field terminal. If the supply and earth are good and there is still no output, the alternator or its regulator is faulty."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Overcharging and noise"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Voltage above about 15 V with the engine running is too high. The battery may boil, lose water and be damaged, and electronics may fail. The cause is usually a faulty regulator, or a poor sensing connection. Replace the regulator or alternator."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["A **squeal** can be a slipping belt or a worn tensioner. Check the belt and its pulleys.","A **grinding or rumbling noise** that changes with engine speed is usually a worn alternator bearing.","A **whine** that varies with load may be a failed diode in the rectifier."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing output and cables"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Use a clamp meter around the alternator output cable, or a carbon-pile load tester, to measure the output current at a set speed with the loads on. Compare the reading with the rated output given in the manual. A reading well below the rating shows a worn alternator."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Measure the voltage drop between the alternator output terminal and the battery positive terminal with the engine running and the loads on. A drop of more than about 0.3 to 0.5 V shows a poor connection or damaged cable. Do the same on the earth side, from the alternator case to the battery negative."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Replacing an alternator"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Disconnect the battery negative terminal first.","Release the belt tension and remove the belt.","Disconnect the wiring and remove the mounting bolts.","Fit the new unit, tighten the bolts to torque and fit the belt with the right tension.","Reconnect the battery and test the output and the warning lamp."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnostic examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: Flat battery every morning.** A car starts well after a charge, but is flat after a night. The alternator gives 14.2 V with the engine running, so charging is good. A parasitic drain test shows 380 mA after 30 minutes, far above 50 mA. Pulling fuses one at a time shows the drain drops to 20 mA when the radio fuse is pulled. The aftermarket amplifier is wired to a permanent supply. It is rewired through a relay switched by the ignition."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: Battery light on at idle only.** The charging light flickers at idle and goes out at higher revs. Voltage at the battery is 12.8 V at idle with the lights on, rising to 14.1 V at 2,000 rpm. The belt is tight, but a voltage drop test finds 0.9 V between the alternator output and the battery positive when loaded. A corroded fuse link is found in the main cable. A new link restores the output."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Battery boils and smells.** The battery needs water often and the case is hot. The system voltage is 15.6 V with the engine running. The regulator is faulty. A new alternator brings the voltage to 14.3 V. The battery is checked and, as it was damaged by the overcharge, replaced."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never disconnect the battery while the engine is running to test the alternator. The voltage can spike and damage electronics. Never short the alternator output to earth."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Before blaming an alternator, check the belt and battery condition. A weak battery can make a good alternator work hard."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l8", "title": "The starting system", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The starter motor turns the engine fast enough to start. It needs a large current from the battery for a short time. This lesson explains the parts and how they work together."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"starter-circuit"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Starter motor and solenoid"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **starter motor** is a powerful DC electric motor. It has a **pinion gear** that engages with the **ring gear** on the flywheel. The **solenoid** is mounted on the starter. When the driver turns the key, a small current goes to the solenoid coil. This does two jobs: it moves a lever that pushes the pinion into mesh with the ring gear, and then it closes heavy contacts that connect the battery to the motor. A **one-way clutch** (overrunning clutch) in the pinion stops the engine driving the starter once it fires."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The control circuit"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Ignition switch (or start button):** sends the start signal.","**Starter relay:** carries the solenoid current so that the ignition switch carries only a small current.","**Neutral safety switch or clutch switch:** stops the starter engaging when the vehicle is in gear.","**Immobiliser:** the ECU may allow starting only when it recognises the key."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Current draw"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The starter draws a large current, often 150 to 300 amps or more for a small engine, and more in the cold or for a diesel. This is why cables are thick and connections must be clean and tight. The voltage at the battery terminals falls while cranking. It should stay above about 9.6 V at normal temperatures."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Job"],"rows":[["Solenoid","Engages the pinion and switches the heavy current"],["Pinion and ring gear","Transfer the turning force to the engine"],["Overrunning clutch","Protects the starter after the engine starts"],["Battery cables","Carry the high current with low resistance"]],"caption":"Starting system parts"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cranking speed"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine needs to be turned fast enough for the fuel and spark to work and for compression to build. A petrol engine usually starts at about 100 rpm or more, and a diesel at 150 rpm or more, though the minimum rises as the weather gets colder. Cold oil is thick and the battery weaker in the cold, so engine speed drops just when more is needed. This is why winter is when marginal batteries fail. Thin, fully synthetic oil helps cold cranking."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Starter power","steps":["A starter draws 180 A while the battery terminal voltage is 10 V.","Electrical power = 10 × 180 = 1,800 W, or 1.8 kW.","That is about 2.4 horsepower from the battery. A battery rated at 60 Ah (60 amp-hours) can supply 180 A for only a short time. A 3-second crank uses 180 × 3 = 540 A·s, which is 0.15 Ah, less than 0.3% of the battery's capacity. Starting does not drain a healthy battery, but many failed starts in a row do."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Starter cables and the control circuit"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The main cable from battery positive to the starter is thick, often 25 to 50 mm², because the current is large and a small voltage drop matters. The earth path is just as important, from the battery negative to the body and engine block. A good earth strap, between the engine and the body, is needed because the engine sits on rubber mounts. Control wiring is thin and uses relays to carry the small current from the ignition switch to the solenoid."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["The driver turns the key or presses the start button. The computer checks that the immobiliser has accepted the key and that the clutch pedal is pressed (manual) or the selector is in park or neutral (automatic).","The ignition switch, or the computer, energises the starter relay.","The relay sends battery voltage to the solenoid small terminal. The solenoid engages the pinion and closes its heavy contacts.","The starter turns the engine until the engine starts and the driver releases the key. Many modern vehicles stop the cranking automatically once the engine has started."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"On a push-button start system, the vehicle must detect the key fob inside the cabin before it permits the start. A flat battery in the fob, a fault in the antenna or interference can stop the start. Most fobs have a backup, such as a slot or a place where the fob can be held, so that the car can be started when the fob battery is flat."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Disconnect the battery negative terminal before removing a starter motor. The main starter terminal is live all the time, and a spanner touching it and the body will cause a large spark and burns."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A starter motor with a poor connection draws less current and cranks slowly, and the battery may seem good. Always test the cables and earth straps before replacing a starter."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l9", "title": "Starter motors in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A starter motor is one of the most heavily loaded parts on a vehicle. It must give very high torque from rest, for a few seconds, many times a day. This lesson explains the types of starter, the sequence of events when it works, and how to test and fit one."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of starter motor"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","How it works","Typical use"],"rows":[["Direct drive","The motor drives the pinion straight","Older and simple starters, with field windings"],["Gear reduction","A small high-speed motor drives the pinion through a planetary gear, which multiplies torque","Most modern cars. Smaller, lighter, and faster cranking."],["Permanent magnet","Permanent magnets replace the field windings","Common in small starters, often with a gear reduction"],["Heavy-duty","Large motor and solenoid, for diesel and trucks","Large engines at 12 or 24 V"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"The sequence when you turn the key"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Current flows through the small terminal to the solenoid. The **pull-in** winding and the **hold-in** winding are energised.","The solenoid plunger moves and a lever pushes the pinion out to mesh with the ring gear. If the teeth do not mesh, a spring lets the pinion wait until the motor moves slightly.","When the plunger reaches the end of its travel, the main contacts close and full battery current flows to the motor. The pull-in winding is shorted out and only the hold-in winding stays energised.","The motor turns the engine. The overrunning clutch locks the pinion to the motor.","When the engine fires and the key is released, the solenoid is released and the return spring withdraws the pinion. The overrunning clutch frees the pinion while the ring gear is driving faster than the starter."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What current should it draw?"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Engine","Typical cranking current"],"rows":[["Small petrol engine","About 100 to 150 A"],["Medium petrol engine","About 150 to 250 A"],["Diesel car or van","About 250 to 400 A"],["Large diesel","300 to 600 A or more, often at 24 V"]],"caption":"Cold weather and heavy oil raise the current. Check the manual for the starter being tested."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Use a clamp meter or the starter test mode on the battery tester to read the current during cranking. A **high current** with slow cranking points to a starter with a fault: a dragging armature, worn bushes or a shorted winding, or an engine that is hard to turn. A **low current** with slow cranking points to resistance in the circuit, such as a corroded cable or a worn solenoid contact. Normal current and good speed means the starter is healthy."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Testing the control circuit"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["With the key at start, measure the voltage at the solenoid small terminal. It should be close to battery voltage. If not, trace back through the relay, the inhibitor or clutch switch, the immobiliser and the ignition switch.","If the small terminal has voltage and the solenoid does not click, the solenoid is faulty or poorly earthed.","If it clicks but the motor does not turn, check the voltage at the main terminal when the key is turned. If the voltage is low, the cable is bad. If the voltage is good, the starter is faulty.","If the starter spins and does not turn the engine, the drive or overrunning clutch is slipping. Inspect the ring gear."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fitting a starter"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Disconnect the battery negative first, since the main terminal is always live.","Mark the cables, remove the bolts and take the starter out, supporting its weight. Clean the mounting face so it is square to the engine.","Compare the new starter with the old one, check the pinion and the number of teeth.","Check the ring gear teeth through the starter opening for wear and damage. A chipped ring gear can destroy a new starter.","Tighten to the specified torque, connect the cables and the small wire securely, and reconnect the battery. Test starting several times."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Stop-start starters"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The starters of stop-start engines work much harder, with perhaps 10 times as many starts. They have stronger brushes, bearings and gears, and often an improved engagement. They must be replaced with the correct part. A normal starter fitted in its place will wear out in a short time."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A good starter is wasted on a bad battery or a bad cable. Test the whole circuit under load before you replace it."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never operate a starter when the pinion is blocked or when the engine is jammed, and never short the solenoid terminals with a tool while the vehicle is in gear. The vehicle can lurch forward."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l10", "title": "Starting faults", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Starting faults are very common, and most come from the battery or its connections. This lesson helps you to diagnose them by symptom."},
      {"t":"h","text":"By symptom"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Symptom","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Slow cranking","Weak battery, high resistance in cables or earth, worn starter, thick oil in cold weather"],["Single click, no crank","Very low battery, poor terminal connection, worn solenoid contacts, seized engine"],["No click, no crank","Flat battery, no start signal (switch, relay, neutral switch, immobiliser), open circuit"],["Spinning without engaging","Faulty drive or one-way clutch, damaged ring gear teeth"],["Starts then keeps cranking","Starter solenoid contacts stuck, ignition switch fault"]],"caption":"Starting faults by symptom"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Test steps"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Check the battery voltage and charge it if it is low.","Turn the key and listen. Do the lights dim? Does the solenoid click?","Measure the voltage at the battery during cranking. If it falls below about 9.6 V, check the battery.","Measure the voltage at the starter's main terminal while cranking. A big difference from the battery shows a drop in the cable.","Check the drop across the earth path, from the starter case and from the engine to the battery negative.","Check the control circuit at the solenoid's small terminal while the key is held at start. If there is no voltage, trace back through the relay and switch."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Cable and earth strap checks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Look for corrosion, loose bolts and damaged cables. Measure voltage drops while cranking. A typical limit is about 0.5 V on the positive side, and about 0.3 V on the earth side. A larger drop means the part needs cleaning or replacing."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Stop-start systems"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Vehicles that stop and restart the engine automatically at traffic lights use stronger starters and EFB or AGM batteries. A **battery monitoring sensor** on the negative terminal tells the ECU about the battery's condition. After fitting a new battery, the ECU often needs to be told, by a procedure using a scan tool. Use the right type of battery and follow the manual, or the stop-start system and the battery will fail early."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Diagnostic examples"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 1: Click only.** A car clicks when the key is turned and does not crank. The battery is 12.5 V and the lights stay bright. This means the battery is good and the solenoid is acting but the heavy current is not passing. Voltage at the starter main terminal during the attempt is 12.4 V, but the starter does not turn. The starter motor is faulty, with worn brushes, and a new starter cures it."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 2: Slow crank when cold.** A diesel van cranks slowly on cold mornings. Voltage at the battery during cranking is 9.1 V and falls from 12.5 V. A load test shows a weak battery. The battery is five years old and its CCA is half the rating. A new battery of the right rating restores normal cranking."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Example 3: Works only after a few tries.** A car starts only after several attempts, with a quiet click on the failed ones. The battery is good. A voltage test on the small terminal shows 12 V on a good attempt and 4 V on a bad one. A voltage drop through the ignition switch is found. A worn switch is replaced."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If a vehicle is slow to crank only when hot, suspect the starter itself or the heat soak around the starter. Check the starter's current draw and the cable condition."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Keep hands, tools and clothing away from belts and fans. Never bridge the starter solenoid terminals with a metal tool while the vehicle is in gear."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m10-l11", "title": "Electrical loads, battery drain and customer accessories", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A battery flat after a night, a slow start after a weekend and a battery that dies after fitting a new accessory are among the most common complaints a workshop sees. They are caused by loads that draw current when they should not. This lesson shows how to measure and find them, and how to size loads and accessories."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Typical loads"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Load","Approximate current at 12 V"],"rows":[["Headlamps, dipped (pair, halogen)","About 9 to 10 A"],["Main beam (pair, halogen)","About 10 to 12 A more"],["Heated rear window","About 10 to 20 A"],["Heater blower, high","About 10 to 20 A"],["Wipers","About 3 to 8 A"],["Fuel pump","About 3 to 8 A"],["Engine cooling fan, one","About 15 to 30 A"],["Radio and amplifier","About 2 to 10 A, more for large amplifiers"]],"caption":"Add up the loads that are on at once and compare with the alternator output at idle."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Is the alternator big enough?","steps":["A car is idling at traffic lights at night with dipped lights (9 A), heated rear window (15 A), blower on high (15 A), wipers (6 A), engine management and fuel pump (10 A) and cooling fan (20 A).","Total load = 9 + 15 + 15 + 6 + 10 + 20 = 75 A.","The alternator gives only about 40 A at idle, so the battery must supply the other 35 A. At 35 A, a 60 Ah battery would last 60 ÷ 35 = 1.7 hours at best. Drivers who repeat this often will find a flat battery.","Advice: turn off the unneeded loads, drive at higher speed for a while, or fit a higher-output alternator if loads have been added."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parasitic drain"},
      {"t":"p","text":"When the ignition is off and the vehicle is locked, current should fall to a very low value. Modern vehicles have many modules that stay awake for a time and then go to sleep, so the drain starts higher and falls over about 10 to 45 minutes. A healthy vehicle usually draws under 50 mA at rest. A drain above about 100 mA flattens a battery in a few days or weeks."},
      {"t":"example","title":"How long can a battery stand a drain?","steps":["A 60 Ah battery that is fully charged has about 30 Ah of usable capacity before it is too flat to start the engine reliably.","With a drain of 50 mA (0.05 A) it lasts 30 ÷ 0.05 = 600 hours, or 25 days.","With a drain of 300 mA, it lasts 30 ÷ 0.3 = 100 hours, or about 4 days.","With a drain of 1 A it lasts 30 hours, or 1 day and a bit. A vehicle that stands for a week with a 300 mA drain will not start."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Measuring and finding a drain"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Switch off all loads, close the doors and take the key out, with the vehicle locked. Wait for the modules to sleep. Do not open doors or switch on lights during the test, or the modules will wake again.","Connect an ammeter in series with the battery negative cable, or use a clamp meter on the negative cable that reads milliamps. Start with the meter on a high range, and move to a lower one when the current is steady.","Read the current after the sleep time given in the manual. If it is more than the limit, find the circuit.","Pull fuses one at a time, noting the current. When it falls, the last fuse removed is the circuit with the drain. Refit each fuse before you pull the next.","Trace the circuit on the diagram. Check each load for a stuck relay, a light left on, a faulty module that will not sleep, a sticky switch or an aftermarket accessory."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Common source of drain","Typical sign"],"rows":[["Boot, glovebox or under-bonnet light staying on","Steady current of 200 to 800 mA"],["Stuck relay","A relay clicks or stays warm with the vehicle off"],["Module that does not sleep","A drain that falls but not to the normal level"],["Aftermarket alarm, tracker, dashcam, amplifier","A drain that drops when the fitted item's fuse or feed is removed"],["Leaking alternator diode","A drain that falls when the alternator connection is removed"],["Damaged wiring or water in a connector","A drain with no obvious load"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fitting accessories safely"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Work out the current of the new device and choose a wire and fuse to suit, as in module 9.","Take the feed from a suitable circuit and use a relay for loads over about 10 A.","For switched devices, use a circuit that is live only with the ignition on. For devices that must stay on, use a permanent feed with an inline fuse, and keep the standby current low.","Fit the fuse close to the power source, and route wiring away from heat and sharp edges, and protected by a grommet where it passes through metal.","Never splice into airbag, ABS or engine-control wiring."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A battery that goes flat and recovers on charge usually has a drain or a charging fault, not a defective battery. Measure before you replace."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Ask the customer what has been fitted recently, and how the vehicle is used. A new alarm, a phone charger left plugged in or a trailer hitch light can be the answer."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Battery, charging and cranking tests","objective":"Test battery state of charge, measure alternator output and ripple, and carry out a cranking voltage-drop test on a training vehicle.","time":"75 minutes","tools":["Digital multimeter, clamp meter and battery conductance tester","Battery charger and safety glasses","Terminal cleaning brush, spanners and a torque wrench","Workshop manual with battery and charging specifications","Notepad and a results table"],"safety":["Wear safety glasses and gloves when handling the battery, and keep sparks and flames away.","Do not disconnect the battery while the engine is running.","Keep hands, hair and leads away from belts and fans.","Do not put a spanner across the battery terminals or any live cable.","Run the engine only where exhaust is extracted."],"steps":["Inspect the battery, terminals and cables and record anything unusual.","Measure the open-circuit voltage after the battery has rested, and note the approximate state of charge.","Test the battery with the conductance tester and record the result.","Start the engine and measure the system voltage at idle with no loads.","Raise the speed to 2,000 rpm and switch on the headlamps, blower and rear heater. Record the voltage.","Set the meter to AC volts and measure the ripple at the battery. Record the value.","Measure the alternator output current with a clamp meter and compare it with the manual.","Disable the fuel or ignition so the engine will not start, and have a helper crank the engine.","Measure the battery voltage while cranking, and the voltage drop on the positive and earth sides.","Compare each reading with the specification and mark each as within limit or not.","Clean and tighten any poor connection, then repeat one test to confirm an improvement.","Write a summary of the battery, charging and starting condition."],"checks":["I measured the battery voltage and described the state of charge.","I measured charging voltage with and without loads, and the AC ripple.","I measured the cranking voltage and the drops on the positive and earth sides.","I followed safe procedures around the battery and the running engine.","I explained what each result means."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m10-q01","q":"How many cells does a 12 V lead-acid battery have?","options":["Four, each giving about 3 V","Twelve, each giving about 1 V","Six, each giving about 2.1 V","Three, each giving about 4 V"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l1","explain":"Six cells in series give about 12.6 V when fully charged."},
    {"id":"m10-q02","q":"What does CCA stand for and what does it show?","options":["Cold-cranking amps, the current the battery can deliver at low temperature","Constant charging amps, the current that is needed to recharge the battery","Capacity in cold amps, the total energy that is stored in the battery","Cell charge average, the average voltage that is found in each cell"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l1","explain":"A higher CCA means more starting power in cold conditions. Diesel engines need more."},
    {"id":"m10-q03","q":"A rested battery reads 12.2 V. About what state of charge does this show?","options":["Flat","About 100%","About 25%","About 50%"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l1","explain":"12.6 V is about full, 12.4 about 75%, 12.2 about 50%, 12.0 about 25%."},
    {"id":"m10-q04","q":"Which gas does a charging battery give off that can explode?","options":["Hydrogen","Oxygen","Carbon monoxide","Nitrogen"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l1","explain":"Hydrogen with a spark can explode. Keep flames and sparks away and charge in a ventilated area."},
    {"id":"m10-q05","q":"What is the correct first action if battery acid splashes in the eyes?","options":["Wait to see whether the stinging stops before doing anything else","Add a little baking soda to the eye to neutralise the acid first","Wipe the eye with a clean cloth and carry on working with the other eye","Rinse with plenty of clean water at once and get medical help"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l1","explain":"Fast, thorough rinsing limits the damage. Get medical help as well."},
    {"id":"m10-q06","q":"What type of battery is commonly used in stop-start vehicles?","options":["A dry-cell torch battery","A flooded battery that needs topping up","A small motorcycle battery","An EFB or AGM battery"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l1","explain":"These batteries cope with repeated deep discharge and recharge."},
    {"id":"m10-q07","q":"What is the voltage of one fully charged lead-acid cell?","options":["About 1.2 V","About 4.2 V","About 2.1 V","About 12.6 V"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l2","explain":"Six cells in series give about 12.6 V. Each cell is about 2.1 V when full."},
    {"id":"m10-q08","q":"What does a hydrometer measure?","options":["The voltage across the battery terminals","The cranking current of the starter","The temperature of the cylinder block","The specific gravity of the electrolyte"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l2","explain":"Specific gravity shows how much acid is in the electrolyte, and so the state of charge. A cell far below the others is faulty."},
    {"id":"m10-q09","q":"Why does a lead-acid battery fail faster in hot climates?","options":["Heat makes the battery acid thicker","Heat corrodes grids and dries cells","Heat makes the plates grow thinner","Heat raises the cold-cranking rating"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l2","explain":"High temperatures accelerate the chemical reactions that destroy the grids, and increase gassing and water loss."},
    {"id":"m10-q10","q":"A battery has one cell with specific gravity 0.050 below the others. What is the fault?","options":["A shorted or damaged cell","The battery is fully charged","The vehicle needs a new alternator","The terminals are too tight"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l2","explain":"A cell that is much lower than the rest has a short between plates or a damaged separator, and the battery will not hold charge. It must be replaced."},
    {"id":"m10-q11","q":"What does CCA stand for?","options":["Charge control ampere","Current capacity average","Cell capacity assessment","Cold-cranking amps"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l2","explain":"CCA is the current a battery can supply at −18 °C for 30 seconds while holding a minimum voltage. It shows starting ability in the cold."},
    {"id":"m10-q12","q":"Why should the last jump lead clamp go to bare metal away from the flat battery?","options":["The donor battery will otherwise overheat and may spill its acid","The metal makes the current flow in the correct direction to the flat battery","A spark there is less likely to ignite hydrogen from the battery","The flat battery needs the earth connection to be as slow as possible"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l3","explain":"Connecting to the flat battery's negative terminal puts the final spark where hydrogen collects."},
    {"id":"m10-q13","q":"What is a typical parasitic drain limit once the modules have gone to sleep?","options":["About 5 A, which is a normal level for a modern vehicle at rest","About 500 mA, because modules never go to sleep fully","About 50 A, which is the level of an engine that is cranking","Below about 50 mA"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l3","explain":"Higher values drain the battery over days. Check the manual."},
    {"id":"m10-q14","q":"How do you measure parasitic drain?","options":["Connect an ammeter in series with the battery negative and wait for modules to sleep","Connect a voltmeter across the battery with the engine running at idle","Hold a clamp meter around the alternator cable and switch the key on","Measure the resistance of the battery cables with the battery in place"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l3","explain":"Current is measured in series. Do not crank the engine with the ammeter connected."},
    {"id":"m10-q15","q":"A battery shows 12.6 V but the voltage falls below 9 V during a load test. What does this suggest?","options":["The battery is fully healthy and ready for another year of service","The battery needs more distilled water adding to the cells","The battery is worn out and cannot deliver current","The alternator is overcharging it and boiling the electrolyte"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l3","explain":"Good voltage with poor load performance shows high internal resistance."},
    {"id":"m10-q16","q":"Why should a frozen battery not be charged?","options":["It can crack or explode","It will not accept any current at all","It will charge too fast","The charger will give the wrong voltage"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l3","explain":"Let it thaw first, and inspect it for damage."},
    {"id":"m10-q17","q":"What liquid should be used to top up a non-sealed battery?","options":["Tap water","Distilled water","Fresh battery acid","Brake fluid"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l3","explain":"Only the water in the electrolyte is lost in use, not the acid. Distilled water avoids adding minerals that harm the plates."},
    {"id":"m10-q18","q":"A 60 Ah battery is missing 45 Ah. How long on a 6 A charger, allowing a factor of 1.3?","options":["About 9.75 hours","About 7.5 hours of charging","About 2.5 hours of charging","About 13 hours of charging"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l4","explain":"Time = 45 ÷ 6 × 1.3 = 9.75 hours. The factor allows for heat and gas losses during charging."},
    {"id":"m10-q19","q":"What does a smart charger do when the battery is full?","options":["Keeps charging at full current","Switches off and never restarts","Drops to a float voltage","Raises the voltage to boost it"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l4","explain":"A float stage gives a low voltage that maintains the charge without overcharging, so the charger can be left connected."},
    {"id":"m10-q20","q":"Why must an AGM battery be charged with a regulated charger?","options":["AGM batteries cannot take any charge","Overcharging can dry the glass mats","AGM batteries must be charged at a high rate","AGM batteries do not need a charger"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l4","explain":"AGM cells recombine gas, so excess voltage damages them. A maximum of about 14.4 to 14.8 V is typical, but follow the maker."},
    {"id":"m10-q21","q":"A battery being charged becomes very hot and smells of rotten eggs. What should you do?","options":["Switch off and let it cool","Increase the charge current","Pour cold water on the battery","Remove the vent caps and leave it running"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l4","explain":"Heat and a sulphur smell show overcharging or an internal short. Stop, let the battery cool, and test it before use."},
    {"id":"m10-q22","q":"What does the rotor in an alternator do?","options":["It stores the electrical charge until the battery needs it","It forms a rotating magnetic field","It changes the AC voltage into a DC voltage for the whole vehicle","It controls the output voltage of the alternator under all loads"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l5","explain":"The rotor is an electromagnet fed through slip rings and brushes."},
    {"id":"m10-q23","q":"What do the rectifier diodes do?","options":["They drive the rotor from the engine by means of a belt and a pulley","They regulate the output voltage of the alternator as the speed changes","They connect the battery to the starter motor while cranking","They change the AC from the stator into DC"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l5","explain":"Six diodes convert the three-phase AC into DC for the vehicle."},
    {"id":"m10-q24","q":"What does the voltage regulator control?","options":["The size of the stator windings as the current rises in them","The speed of the engine, so that the alternator turns at the same rate","The current in the rotor field winding, to hold the output voltage steady","The amount of fuel injected by the injectors under all loads"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l5","explain":"More field current gives a stronger magnetic field and so more output; less field current lowers the output. This holds the voltage steady as the load and engine speed change."},
    {"id":"m10-q25","q":"What is a typical charging voltage with the engine running?","options":["About 12.0 V, which is the voltage of a rested battery","About 13.8 to 14.7 V","About 9 to 10 V, which is the usual level while the starter cranks","About 18 to 20 V, which is the level that is used by diesel trucks"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l5","explain":"Check the manual. Some systems vary the voltage to save fuel."},
    {"id":"m10-q26","q":"High AC ripple at the battery with the engine running suggests what?","options":["A loose belt on the water pump","A failed diode in the rectifier","A faulty fuel pump","A weak battery that needs charging"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l5","explain":"The rectified output should be smooth. A diode fault leaves too much AC."},
    {"id":"m10-q27","q":"An engine idles at 750 rpm. The crank pulley is 150 mm and the alternator pulley is 60 mm. How fast does the alternator turn?","options":["300 rpm","750 rpm","1,875 rpm","4,688 rpm"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l6","explain":"The ratio is 150 ÷ 60 = 2.5. The alternator turns at 750 × 2.5 = 1,875 rpm."},
    {"id":"m10-q28","q":"Why is the output of an alternator at idle much less than its rated output?","options":["The regulator limits it at idle","The battery is not connected at idle","The diodes only work above idle","It is rated at a higher speed"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l6","explain":"Output rises with alternator speed. A rating is quoted at a high speed, and at idle it gives perhaps 30 to 50% of it."},
    {"id":"m10-q29","q":"On a vehicle with smart charging, the battery voltage reads 12.6 V on one test and 14.9 V on another. Is it faulty?","options":["Not necessarily. The computer varies it","Yes, because the battery is always faulty here","Yes, so the alternator must be replaced now","No, because the meter must be broken"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l6","explain":"Smart charging lowers the voltage during acceleration and raises it during deceleration to recover energy. Check with a scan tool."},
    {"id":"m10-q30","q":"What does an AC ripple reading above about 0.5 V on the battery suggest?","options":["A weak starter motor on the engine","A blocked radiator at the front","A failed diode in the alternator","A broken spark plug lead on top"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l6","explain":"The rectifier smooths the output. A failed diode leaves an AC ripple that shows up on an AC voltage reading."},
    {"id":"m10-q31","q":"An alternator belt squeals when the engine is revved. What is the most likely cause?","options":["A loose or worn belt or tensioner","A burnt-out headlamp bulb that has increased the load","A blocked air filter that is restricting the intake air","A failed battery that cannot hold a charge for more than a day"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l7","explain":"Check the belt condition and tension before blaming the alternator."},
    {"id":"m10-q32","q":"A charging voltage reading of 15.8 V with the engine running suggests what?","options":["A faulty regulator causing overcharging","A flat battery that is pulling the voltage down at the terminals","A broken belt that is stopping the alternator from turning","A perfectly normal charging system that is working as designed"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l7","explain":"Overcharging can boil the battery and damage electronics."},
    {"id":"m10-q33","q":"Why must you never disconnect the battery to test an alternator with the engine running?","options":["The alternator will stop turning because it needs the battery for its drive","The battery will charge faster once the alternator is on its own","The engine will stall at once because the battery feeds the starter","Voltage spikes can damage the electronic systems"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l7","explain":"Modern vehicles have sensitive electronics that depend on the battery to smooth voltage."},
    {"id":"m10-q34","q":"A car is flat every morning but charges well when driven. What is the first test?","options":["A compression test on each cylinder","A wheel alignment on the front axle","An oil pressure test on the engine","A parasitic drain test"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l7","explain":"A flat battery overnight with good charging points to current drawn when off. Measure the drain after the modules have slept."},
    {"id":"m10-q35","q":"What system voltage with the engine running shows overcharging?","options":["Between 13.8 and 14.7 V","Above about 15 V","Between 12 and 13 V","Below 12 V"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l7","explain":"A voltage above about 15 V shows a regulator or sensing fault. The battery boils and loses water, and electronics can be damaged."},
    {"id":"m10-q36","q":"Which part carries the large current from the battery to the starter motor?","options":["The thick battery cable, through the solenoid contacts","The thin ignition switch wire that runs to the solenoid control terminal","The alternator belt that drives the starter pinion","The ECU signal wire that tells the starter when to turn"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l8","explain":"The small control current only operates the solenoid."},
    {"id":"m10-q37","q":"What does the solenoid on a starter do?","options":["It measures the battery voltage and cuts off when it is too low","It engages the pinion and closes the contacts that supply the motor","It reduces the speed of the starter motor once it is turning","It generates the electricity that the starter motor needs to turn the engine"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l8","explain":"It combines a mechanical and an electrical function."},
    {"id":"m10-q38","q":"What does the overrunning clutch do?","options":["It allows the starter to engage in neutral and nowhere else","It stops the running engine from driving the starter","It keeps the pinion in mesh after the engine has started up","It stops the starter from drawing too much current from the battery"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l8","explain":"Without it, the starter would be spun to a destructive speed when the engine fires."},
    {"id":"m10-q39","q":"What is the purpose of a neutral safety switch?","options":["To switch on the reversing lights when the gear is selected","To stop the starter working while the vehicle is in gear","To reduce the starter current when the battery is cold","To switch off the engine when the vehicle stops at traffic lights"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l8","explain":"It prevents a vehicle from lurching forward when it is started in gear."},
    {"id":"m10-q40","q":"Why should the battery negative terminal be disconnected before removing a starter motor?","options":["The ECU will lose its memory forever if the battery is left on","The main starter terminal is always live and a short circuit could cause burns or fire","The battery will charge faster when it is out of the circuit","The starter will not come out of the engine bay unless the battery is off"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l8","explain":"A spanner across the live terminal and the body makes a short circuit."},
    {"id":"m10-q41","q":"A starter draws 180 A at 10 V. What is its electrical power?","options":["18 W","180 W","1,800 W","18,000 W"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l8","explain":"Power = voltage × current = 10 × 180 = 1,800 W, or 1.8 kW."},
    {"id":"m10-q42","q":"What does the overrunning clutch in a starter do?","options":["It engages the pinion with the ring gear","It switches the heavy current to the motor","It limits the current taken by the motor","It stops the engine driving the starter"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l9","explain":"Without it the ring gear would spin the starter at a destructive speed. It frees the pinion once the engine runs faster than the starter."},
    {"id":"m10-q43","q":"Slow cranking with low current draw suggests what?","options":["A worn starter armature that drags","High resistance in the circuit","A seized engine that will not turn","A shorted starter winding inside"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l9","explain":"High current with slow cranking points to the starter or engine. Low current means something limits it in the circuit."},
    {"id":"m10-q44","q":"The solenoid clicks but the starter motor does not turn and the main terminal has good voltage. What is faulty?","options":["The starter motor itself","The ignition switch contacts","The clutch pedal switch contacts","The neutral safety switch contacts"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l9","explain":"If the main terminal has full voltage when the key is turned and the motor does not turn, the fault is in the motor."},
    {"id":"m10-q45","q":"Why should the ring gear be checked when a starter is replaced?","options":["The ring gear sets the starter torque value","The ring gear controls the engine oil pressure","The ring gear keeps the battery charged","A damaged ring gear can ruin the starter"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l9","explain":"Chipped or worn teeth cause jamming and noise, which damage the new pinion. Inspect the teeth through the starter opening."},
    {"id":"m10-q46","q":"The starter makes a single click and the engine does not turn. The battery is fully charged. What do you check next?","options":["The fuel pressure at the fuel rail and the spark at each plug lead","The condition of the spark plugs and the ignition coils in the engine","The cabin air filter and the blower motor for the heating system","Voltage at the starter terminal while cranking, and the cable and earth connections"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l10","explain":"A big drop in the cables or a bad solenoid contact is a common cause."},
    {"id":"m10-q47","q":"The starter motor spins but does not turn the engine. What is the most likely fault?","options":["A flat battery that cannot supply the large current for cranking","A blown fuse in the ignition circuit that cuts off the starter signal","A faulty starter drive or one-way clutch, or a damaged ring gear","A faulty alternator that is not charging the battery while driving"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l10","explain":"The starter turns freely, so the problem is in the transfer of motion."},
    {"id":"m10-q48","q":"What is a typical limit for voltage drop on the positive side while cranking?","options":["About 0.5 V","About 0 V exactly","About 5 V","About 12 V"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l10","explain":"Check the manual. A larger drop means a poor connection or damaged cable."},
    {"id":"m10-q49","q":"What should be done after fitting a new battery to a vehicle with a battery monitoring sensor?","options":["Nothing, because the system updates itself the very first time it is used","Disconnect the monitoring sensor permanently so that it cannot interfere","Register the new battery with the vehicle using a scan tool, if the manual requires","Replace the alternator at the same time to match the new battery"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l10","explain":"The system needs the new battery's details for correct charging."},
    {"id":"m10-q50","q":"Slow cranking only in very cold weather is most likely due to what?","options":["A broken alternator belt that slips when the engine is turned over","Reduced battery output and thick engine oil","A blown headlamp bulb that has increased the load on the battery","A weak fuel pump that cannot supply enough fuel to start the engine"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l10","explain":"Cold reduces the battery's capacity and makes the engine harder to turn."},
    {"id":"m10-q51","q":"What is the best way to find a faulty starter cable connection?","options":["Measure its resistance with the power on","Hold the cable and feel if it is hot after an hour","Measure the voltage drop across it while cranking","Replace it without testing"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l10","explain":"A drop test shows resistance under load, which is when it matters."},
    {"id":"m10-q52","q":"A vehicle draws 300 mA at rest. About how long will 30 Ah of usable battery capacity last?","options":["About 100 hours","About 10 hours","About 1,000 hours","About 9,000 hours"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l11","explain":"Time = 30 ÷ 0.3 = 100 hours, about 4 days. A vehicle left for a week will not start."},
    {"id":"m10-q53","q":"What is a normal parasitic drain on a modern vehicle at rest?","options":["About 5 A, as with the lights on","Under about 50 mA once asleep","About 1 A, as with a heater on","About 500 mA, as with a radio on"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m10-l11","explain":"Memory, clocks and the alarm need a small current. Anything much above 50 to 100 mA will flatten a battery in days."},
    {"id":"m10-q54","q":"Why must you keep the doors closed during a parasitic drain test?","options":["The ammeter gives false readings in daylight","The doors make the battery heavier","Opening a door wakes the modules","The key must be kept in the lock"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l11","explain":"Doors, lights and other actions wake the modules. Wait for them to sleep again, which can take many minutes."},
    {"id":"m10-q55","q":"Total electrical load at idle is 75 A and the alternator gives about 40 A. What happens?","options":["The battery covers the shortfall","The alternator output rises to 75 A at once","The extra current is returned to the fuel tank","The engine switches off by itself"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m10-l11","explain":"The battery covers the difference. If it continues, the battery will discharge, which is why the loads should be reduced at idle."},
    {"id":"m10-q56","q":"When fitting an accessory, where should its fuse be placed?","options":["Next to the accessory it protects","Under the dashboard in any place","Close to the power source","On the earth wire of the circuit"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m10-l11","explain":"A fuse protects the wire between it and the power source. If it is far from the source, the wire in between is unprotected."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m10-c01","front":"How many cells in a 12 V lead-acid battery?","back":"Six cells of about 2.1 V each.","lesson":"m10-l1"},
    {"id":"m10-c02","front":"What does CCA mean?","back":"Cold-cranking amps: the current a battery can deliver at low temperature.","lesson":"m10-l1"},
    {"id":"m10-c03","front":"What is a fully charged rested battery voltage?","back":"About 12.6 V or more.","lesson":"m10-l1"},
    {"id":"m10-c04","front":"What gas does a charging battery give off?","back":"Hydrogen, which is explosive.","lesson":"m10-l1"},
    {"id":"m10-c05","front":"What is an EFB battery?","back":"An enhanced flooded battery used in many stop-start vehicles.","lesson":"m10-l1"},
    {"id":"m10-c06","front":"Specific gravity of a full, 50% and flat battery?","back":"About 1.265 to 1.280 for full, 1.190 for 50% and 1.120 or less for flat.","lesson":"m10-l2"},
    {"id":"m10-c07","front":"What is CCA?","back":"Cold-cranking amps: the current a battery gives at −18 °C for 30 seconds above a set voltage.","lesson":"m10-l2"},
    {"id":"m10-c08","front":"Name four ways a battery fails.","back":"Sulphation, grid corrosion, shedding paste and a shorted cell.","lesson":"m10-l2"},
    {"id":"m10-c09","front":"Battery capacity at 0 °C and −18 °C?","back":"About 65 to 70% at 0 °C and 40 to 50% at −18 °C.","lesson":"m10-l2"},
    {"id":"m10-c10","front":"How do you jump start safely?","back":"Red to flat positive, red to donor positive, black to donor negative, last black to bare metal on the flat car.","lesson":"m10-l3"},
    {"id":"m10-c11","front":"What is parasitic drain?","back":"Current that flows with the vehicle switched off.","lesson":"m10-l3"},
    {"id":"m10-c12","front":"What is a typical normal parasitic drain?","back":"Below about 50 mA once the modules have slept.","lesson":"m10-l3"},
    {"id":"m10-c13","front":"What does a battery load test show?","back":"Whether the battery can deliver current without the voltage falling too low.","lesson":"m10-l3"},
    {"id":"m10-c14","front":"Formula for charge time?","back":"Hours = Ah to replace ÷ charge current × 1.3.","lesson":"m10-l4"},
    {"id":"m10-c15","front":"What are the three stages of a smart charger?","back":"Bulk, absorption and float.","lesson":"m10-l4"},
    {"id":"m10-c16","front":"Charging voltage limit for AGM?","back":"About 14.4 to 14.8 V, as the maker says. Use an AGM mode.","lesson":"m10-l4"},
    {"id":"m10-c17","front":"What is the rotor of an alternator?","back":"A rotating electromagnet fed through slip rings and brushes.","lesson":"m10-l5"},
    {"id":"m10-c18","front":"What is the stator?","back":"The fixed windings in which the AC voltage is made.","lesson":"m10-l5"},
    {"id":"m10-c19","front":"What do the rectifier diodes do?","back":"They change the AC to DC.","lesson":"m10-l5"},
    {"id":"m10-c20","front":"What does the regulator do?","back":"It controls field current to keep the output voltage steady.","lesson":"m10-l5"},
    {"id":"m10-c21","front":"What is typical charging voltage?","back":"About 13.8 to 14.7 V.","lesson":"m10-l5"},
    {"id":"m10-c22","front":"How do you find alternator speed?","back":"Engine speed times the pulley ratio, crank pulley diameter over alternator pulley diameter.","lesson":"m10-l6"},
    {"id":"m10-c23","front":"Typical alternator output at idle?","back":"Only about 30 to 50% of the rated output.","lesson":"m10-l6"},
    {"id":"m10-c24","front":"What is smart charging?","back":"The computer raises the voltage on deceleration and lowers it on acceleration to save fuel.","lesson":"m10-l6"},
    {"id":"m10-c25","front":"What does high AC ripple suggest?","back":"A failed rectifier diode.","lesson":"m10-l7"},
    {"id":"m10-c26","front":"What does overcharging look like?","back":"Above about 15 V with the engine running, often from a faulty regulator.","lesson":"m10-l7"},
    {"id":"m10-c27","front":"Why not disconnect the battery with the engine running?","back":"Voltage spikes can damage the electronics.","lesson":"m10-l7"},
    {"id":"m10-c28","front":"What does a system voltage above 15 V mean?","back":"Overcharging, usually a regulator or sensing fault.","lesson":"m10-l7"},
    {"id":"m10-c29","front":"What does the starter solenoid do?","back":"It engages the pinion and closes the heavy current contacts.","lesson":"m10-l8"},
    {"id":"m10-c30","front":"What does the overrunning clutch do?","back":"It protects the starter once the engine fires.","lesson":"m10-l8"},
    {"id":"m10-c31","front":"How much current does a starter draw?","back":"Often 150 to 300 amps or more.","lesson":"m10-l8"},
    {"id":"m10-c32","front":"What does the neutral safety switch do?","back":"It stops the starter working when the vehicle is in gear.","lesson":"m10-l8"},
    {"id":"m10-c33","front":"Cranking speed needed to start?","back":"About 100 rpm for a petrol engine and 150 rpm for a diesel, higher in the cold.","lesson":"m10-l8"},
    {"id":"m10-c34","front":"What does a starter solenoid do?","back":"It pushes the pinion into the ring gear and closes the heavy contacts to the motor.","lesson":"m10-l9"},
    {"id":"m10-c35","front":"High current and slow cranking?","back":"A fault in the starter, such as drag or shorted windings, or a hard-to-turn engine.","lesson":"m10-l9"},
    {"id":"m10-c36","front":"Low current and slow cranking?","back":"High resistance in a cable, earth or the solenoid contacts.","lesson":"m10-l9"},
    {"id":"m10-c37","front":"What does a single click and no crank suggest?","back":"Very low battery, poor connection or worn solenoid contacts.","lesson":"m10-l10"},
    {"id":"m10-c38","front":"What does spinning without engaging suggest?","back":"A faulty starter drive or damaged ring gear.","lesson":"m10-l10"},
    {"id":"m10-c39","front":"What is a typical lowest cranking voltage?","back":"Above about 9.6 V at normal temperature.","lesson":"m10-l10"},
    {"id":"m10-c40","front":"What drop is typical on the starter positive side?","back":"About 0.5 V or less.","lesson":"m10-l10"},
    {"id":"m10-c41","front":"What special battery do stop-start vehicles need?","back":"An EFB or AGM battery, registered with the vehicle if required.","lesson":"m10-l10"},
    {"id":"m10-c42","front":"Normal parasitic drain?","back":"Under about 50 mA after the modules have gone to sleep.","lesson":"m10-l11"},
    {"id":"m10-c43","front":"How do you find a drain?","back":"Measure in series at the battery negative, then pull fuses one at a time until the current drops.","lesson":"m10-l11"},
    {"id":"m10-c44","front":"How long does a 30 Ah reserve last at 300 mA?","back":"30 ÷ 0.3 = 100 hours, or about 4 days.","lesson":"m10-l11"}
  ]
});
